title: Tag subsumption and exclusivity
tags: [[Hierarchical tags]]
kind: reference
aliases: subsumption
summary: A specific tag implies its ancestors, a general negative covers its descendants, and an exclusive tag pushes its siblings out — mf tag keeps records written that way.

A path is read as an implication: `musique/jazz` ''is'' music. So a record carries the
most specific thing known about it, and `mf tag` rewrites it to stay so:

* `add P` — the record gains `tag` → P and loses its `tag` refs to P's ''ancestors'', which P already says. It keeps the ones to P's descendants (`musique/jazz/bebop` says more than `musique/jazz`).
* `deny P` — the record gains `negative_tag` → P and loses its `negative_tag` refs to P's ''descendants'', which "not P" already says.
* ''Contradictions'' — each answer also takes back the opposite answers it makes false. Having P means wholly having every ancestor of P, so `add P` removes the `negative_tag` and `mixed_tag` refs to P and to its ancestors. Not having P means having none of its descendants, so `deny P` removes the `tag` and `mixed_tag` refs to P and to its descendants (a mixed mark is "partly", which a whole answer settles). Conversely `mixed P` — partly P, partly not — removes the `tag` refs to P and to its descendants (wholly jazz would be wholly music) and the `negative_tag` refs to P and to its ancestors (wholly not music would be wholly not jazz). The rest is compatible and stays: "not bebop" beside jazz, "music" beside "not jazz".
* ''Exclusivity'' — when P is exclusive, `add P` also removes the `tag` refs to P's siblings. P is exclusive when it carries `exclusive = true`, or when its parent carries `partition = true` (all the parent's children are then exclusive of one another).
* `remove P` rewrites nothing else.

The rewrite is per record, over the whole selection, in the order: add the ref, drop what
it subsumes, drop what it contradicts, drop the siblings — each a single set write
([[Editing a set of metarecords]]).
Adding a ref the record already has writes nothing ([[No duplicate rows]]), so running
the same `add` twice leaves the rows, ids included, as they were.

So a record never holds two answers that contradict each other, and changing an answer —
`deny` or `mixed` where `add` was given, as `gui-tag-folder.sh --redo` allows — leaves
nothing of the old one.

The classification flow asks its questions in the same terms: a question is not asked
about a tag already implied by a more specific positive, about anything under a general
negative, or about a sibling an exclusive tag has ruled out (`gui-tag-next.sh`,
[[Classifying with tags]]).
